So mask reflectivity is not merely an optical-quality statistic.

When EUV is the bottleneck, reflectivity can become a capacity variable.

## 4. The absorber creates the actual pattern

The multilayer mirror by itself would simply reflect EUV everywhere.

The circuit pattern comes from an **absorber layer** patterned on top of the mirror.

Where the absorber remains, EUV reflection is strongly suppressed.

Where the absorber has been removed, the multilayer can reflect light toward the projection optics.

So the scanner sees a spatial pattern of reflected versus suppressed EUV energy and projects that image onto the photoresist-coated wafer.

This is the EUV version of a stencil — but the stencil is sitting on top of a sophisticated optical mirror.

## 5. Why mask defects can hide below the surface

EUV introduces a particularly difficult defect problem.

A particle or bump can be buried inside or underneath the multilayer while the mask blank is being manufactured.

Even if the top surface later looks smooth, that buried defect can deform the multilayer stack above it.

Because the multilayer controls the phase and direction of reflected EUV light, a tiny deformation can alter the aerial image that reaches the wafer.

That means the fab does not only care about obvious absorber defects on the surface.

It also cares about **multilayer defects that change the reflected wavefront**.

This is more subtle than a simple blocked-or-unblocked stencil.

## 6. Why inspection is unusually hard

A defect matters only if it changes the printed wafer image enough to hurt yield.

But inspecting an EUV mask with a different wavelength can miss behavior that appears specifically at 13.5 nm.

That is why **actinic inspection** is important: inspecting with EUV-wavelength light so the measurement sees the mask more like the production scanner does.

The tradeoff is familiar by now.

More inspection improves confidence and defect capture, but inspection tools themselves must be precise, fast and productive enough for manufacturing.

The real goal is not “inspect everything infinitely.”

It is to detect printable defects before a reusable master pattern repeats them across expensive wafers.

## 7. The mask is a system, not one supplier product

Several specialized capabilities have to work together:

- an ultra-flat, low-thermal-expansion substrate;
- extremely uniform Mo/Si multilayer deposition;
- a protective capping layer;
- absorber deposition and patterning;
- mask writing;
- defect inspection and metrology;
- cleaning and repair when possible;
- pellicle integration and contamination control.

That is why the mask ecosystem should not be reduced to “who sells the blank?”

The economic value may sit in different places depending on which step limits defectivity, throughput or qualification.

KLA participates in mask inspection and metrology. ASML must make the reticle behave correctly inside the scanner. Specialized materials and mask manufacturers contribute blanks, coatings and finished masks.

The key question is the same as elsewhere in the fab:

**which capability is difficult to qualify, scarce enough to constrain output, and valuable enough to capture economics?**

## 8. High-NA makes mask behavior even more important

Lesson 026 showed that High-NA EUV uses different imaging geometry and a half-sized exposure field.

Higher numerical aperture also makes the interaction between light and the three-dimensional mask structure more demanding.

The absorber is not infinitely thin. EUV arrives at an angle, so absorber height, material choice and feature orientation can change the reflected image.

These are often called **mask 3D effects**.

As lithography pushes tighter dimensions, engineers therefore optimize not only the scanner optics, but also absorber materials and mask structure to preserve image quality.

A better scanner can expose weaknesses elsewhere in the patterning system.

